<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Joe Powell had served for six years in the=
 South Staffordshire Regiment when, in December 1892, he was signed by Roya=
l Arsenal (as they were then called), having impressed in a friendly agains=
t them. A powerful and reliable right-back, Powell was soon named captain a=
nd led the side into their first season of league football, after the club,=
 changing its name to Woolwich Arsenal, was accepted into the Second Divisi=
on in 1893. In total, he played 92 games for the club.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">As well as Division Two, Arsenal joined th=
e United League when it was founded in 1896 to give clubs in southern and c=
entral England midweek fixtures alongside their regular Saturday games in t=
he Football League. It was in a United League game away at Kettering Town o=
n 23 November 1896 that Powell suffered a badly broken wrist. The wound bec=
ame infected and he suffered blood poisoning, with symptoms of tetanus. So =
ill he couldn=E2=80=99t go to the hospital, surgeons visited him at home in=
 Plumstead and amputated the arm. It was too late. Infection had set in and=
 Powell died on 29 November, aged 26. An inquest returned a verdict of =E2=
=80=9Caccidental death=E2=80=9D; nobody was to blame.</p>
